  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/8/8a/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_112.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Two pages of the Human Torch showing off for his fans in Glenville get nary a reaction from any of them, and Johnny begins to wonder what's wrong.&nbsp; He returns home only to have Sue show him the nasty things a TV news commentator named Ted Braddock has been saying about him, putting it out that he considers Johnny little more than a \"grandstanding kid.\"\n\nSoon afterward, the police arrive at the house to tell Johnny that they still believe he's okay -- that they don't agree with the commentator -- but that Braddock \"is turning the public against you!\"&nbsp; Johnny rushes to see Braddock in the middle of the broadcast and attempts to defend himself, but Braddock is able to goad Johnny into flaming on, leaving viewers with the impression that he's a hot-head.&nbsp; Even Johnny begins to doubt himself, wondering if he has any right to \"monkey around with crime-fighting,\" simply because an accident granted him his flame powers.\n\nThat evening, a new menace called the \"Eel\" breaks into the lab of the famous inventor Charles Lawson and steals a small case labeled \"Project X.\"&nbsp; Lawson attempts to stop the theft, but the Eel's outfit renders him \"as slippery as the creature you're named after!\"&nbsp; He tries to warn the Eel about the content of the case, but his loud objections over the bag's theft only convince the criminal that it \"must be worth <i>thousands</i>!\"&nbsp; We learn that his uniform is also equipped to give off a strong electric current, very much like an electric eel.\n\nWe soon learn that the Eel is Leopold Stryke, the caretaker of a nearby aquarium, located on a \"deserted beach.\"&nbsp; When he returns to the aquarium, he pries open the bag to reveal a mysterious mechanism, one with a lever on its side.&nbsp; Deciding against flipping the switch, he instead sets out to find a way to fence what he's certain is a valuable piece of technology.\n\nMeanwhile, Lawson reports the theft, and news reports immediately circulate that the device is a \"miniature radioactive atomic pile,\" and that, once opened and exposed to air, it will explode in an atomic blast that can only be averted by pressing a lever -- the very one that the Eel decided <i>not</i> to press!&nbsp; This effectively turns him into a \"living bomb.\"\n\nWhen the Eel approaches his \"fence,\" a man named Moxie Gahagan (who has heard the news broadcast), he asks the Eel if he pressed the lever.&nbsp; The Eel is surprised that Moxie even <i>knows</i> about the theft, but Moxie explains he heard about it on the news, and he urges the Eel to get far away \"before you blow up the whole neighborhood!\"&nbsp; The device could explode at any moment now!\n\nA desperate manhunt ensues as the population tries to track down the menace of the \"Living Bomb!\"&nbsp; Reed tries to convince Johnny to join up with the team so they can join in on the hunt, but the Human Torch decides there's no time to lose!&nbsp; \"I've got to find the Eel <i>now</i>!\"&nbsp; News of the theft has even reached the prisons, where even brilliant men like the Wizard volunteer to help track the local threat (but are refused).\n\nTorch flies to visit Charles Lawson, who helps track down the Eel, thanks to a remnant of the slippery substance that rubbed off the Eel's uniform.&nbsp; He's able to build a tracker, which he gives to the Human Torch, urging him to fly out immediately to stop the threat.&nbsp; But it leads him instead to his own teammate, the Thing, who is carrying a hand-written note by the Eel, which he guesses is what the device sensed.&nbsp; Sadly, in the process, the tracking mechanism has been destroyed.\n\nFor his own part, the Eel has, in the meantime, found some \"lonely woods\" in which to leave the device and flee the scene before it explodes.&nbsp; He plans to escape in a helicopter, but the Human Torch spots him in the air, urging him to hand over the device so that he can get it away from the area.&nbsp; The Eel claims to have already taken care of it, and orders him to leave \"before I snuff you and your flames out forever!\"\n\nWhen the Torch tries to stop the Eel, the criminal whips out another of the things he stole from Professor Lawson's home -- the \"aqua-attractor gun\" which draws moisture out of things, in this case, the nearby clouds.&nbsp; The Torch has to flee before being enveloped by the moist vapors drawn from the clouds.\n\nBut at the same time, the Eel has been forced to land, and when the Torch returns to demand the whereabouts of the bomb, the Eel instead aims the gun at the ground, and in seconds, moisture from the ground temporarily extinguishes Johnny's flame, and he's unable to physically assault the Eel due to the electric current that runs through the Eel's uniform.&nbsp; But as the moisture only dampens Johnny's flame for a moment, he ultimately regains his ability to flame on and melts the gun before it can be used again.\n\nRealizing he's been defeated, the Eel tries instead to convince the Human Torch to join him in his criminal endeavors so that \"we'll grab all of Earth's wealth together!\"&nbsp; The Torch scoffs at the idea, and when the police soon arrive, the Eel finally relents and reveals the bomb's location, not realizing that it wasn't as \"lonely\" as he thought --\"there's a <i>veteran's hospital</i> near those woods!\"\n\nThe Torch races off to see what he can do to avert disaster, as it's now far too late to evacuate the hospital.&nbsp; He finds the device and flies off, knowing that it could explode at any moment.&nbsp; The Torch races upward, ultimately attempting to draw all the heat from the explosion into his own body.\n\nSeconds later, [SOUNDEFFECT: text=WROOOOOM!]\n\nTorch pulls the heat of the explosion skyward instead of toward the nearby veterans' hospital.&nbsp; But doing so costs him all his flame, and the rest of the FF have to save him from plummeting to his death.&nbsp; The team despairs that Johnny may have been killed in the attempt and races to get him to Reed's lab in time to prevent his death by using a device he's been working on that \"may be able to restore half-dead cells to full vitality!\"&nbsp; The others can only watch as Reed works desperately to save the Human Torch.\n\nMeanwhile, TV commentator Braddock has now changed his tune, seeing as how his own <i>son</i> was a patient at the hospital that was just saved by the Human Torch.&nbsp; \"I was just a stubborn opinionated bigot!&nbsp; ... said things about him which I'll always regret!\"&nbsp; His entire attitude toward teenagers seems to have been affected by what Johnny Storm did.\n\nBut the news from the FF is still not good.&nbsp; Johnny is hanging on by just a thread.&nbsp; Citizens mourn while the underworld rejoices, but finally, it turns out he'll pull through, prompting both celebrations...and fury.\n\nBen even shows up at the house where Johnny is recuperating, just to start a friendly fight.&nbsp; For her part, Sue doesn't even try to stop it, happy that everything seems to be getting back to normal again!",
  "grade_one_liner": "An admittedly dumb, contrived plot that somehow delivers enough charm and character beats to make the read unexpectedly pleasant -- even <i>delightful</i> in spots.",
  "subtitle": "Charm Amidst an Hour-Long Crisis",
  "review_text": "A surprisingly satisfying issue that nevertheless has serious problems, interspersed with some genuinely nice moments -- enough, surprisingly, to keep the whole thing afloat.\n\nTo start with the more mundane, the story immediately stages a straw man in the television commentator Braddock, whose only apparent purpose in the story was to show Braddock changing his tune at the end.&nbsp; He's less a character than a plot device, wheeled in solely to manufacture a public‑opinion crisis.&nbsp; And that's to say <i>nothing</i> of the unimaginative villain, the Eel, who also seems to exist only to create a crisis that the Human Torch must solve.\n\n[ASSET: right | ProjectX | width=12%] There were several other silly elements, such as the idea of a \"Project X\" bag which contains a mechanism that requires a lever to be flipped to avoid it turning into an atomic bomb.&nbsp; It's the kind of contrivance that feels like it was dreamed up five minutes before deadline.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=error; text=Is there anyone alive who dares argue for this being a good design choice?&nbsp; <b><i>Come on!</i></b>]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(But the sheer <i>audacity</i> of the silliness caused a momentary chuckle nonetheless.)]\n\nOne might also ask:&nbsp; Why is an atomic bomb called \"Project X?\"\n[BR] Doesn't the bag at least have a <i>warning label</i>?&nbsp; (This Lawson inventor dude needs <i>serious</i> supervision!)\n\nAnd then there's the Eel's headquarters -- an aquarium on a \"deserted beach\" -- and this is on <i>Long Island</i>.&nbsp; <b>Is</b> there an actual deserted beach to be found in such a populated area?&nbsp; Why would an aquarium be in a location that is otherwise deserted?&nbsp; This is the sort of location that only exists in comics -- and only for <i>villains</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(These are questions best left <i>unposed</i>, I suspect --let's not <i>overthink</i> the material!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | DesperateManhunt | width=13%] I also found it quite entertaining that \"one of the greatest manhunts in all history\" began the moment news of the theft of \"Project X\" was announced.\n[BR]\n[BR] The panel makes it seem as though the police, army, navy and secret service could all be called into alert status to track down the so-called \"Living Bomb\" -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=and pull it all off within an <b>hour</b>!]\n[BR]\n[BR] This is 1960s Marvel time at its <i>finest</i>! It's as if the entire Eastern Seaboard mobilized before anyone finished the broadcast!\n\n[ASSET: right | ThingsLongSpeech | width=6%] My eyeballs also threatened to pop out at the one panel on page 7 where the Thing's head is almost squeezed out of it, the one where he makes probably <i>his longest speech ever</i>!\n[BR]\n[BR] This struck me as strangely out of character, as he's normally the one telling Reed to shut his <i>trap</i>.\n\nNot only that, but the Thing also reveals that the Fantastic Four have already had time to visit police headquarters and retrieve a note written by the Eel himself!&nbsp; Talk about <i>time compression</i>!\n\nBut... [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=bold; text=funny thing:]\n[BR] despite the abject (and compounded) silliness of the material, I still found myself strangely drawn to continue...\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | NobleTorch | width=14%] When Johnny realizes how little time he has left to stop the bomb, he understands that the attempt may cost him his own life, but he nobly thinks of the lives of the veterans in the threatened hospital, who \"risked <i>their</i> lives while defending <i>ours</i>!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] His determination to try and save them is probably the first true bit of altruism that the Human Torch has ever shown, especially given that he is a mere teenager.&nbsp; It's the first moment where Johnny feels less like a show‑off and more like a hero.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=Brief though it was, it was still a nice character beat.]\n\nNaturally, the effort of drawing the heat of the explosion away from the veterans' hospital takes every ounce of Johnny's ability, leaving him unconscious in midair.\n[ASSET: center | DrawingOutTheHeat | width=67%]\n\nOnly the arrival of the rest of the Fantastic Four prevents him from falling to his death.\n\n[ASSET: left | ThingsAffection | width=13%] The reactions of the Thing on pages 11 and 13 were also decent:&nbsp; he's genuinely shaken when Johnny's life is in danger, only to immediately \"withdraw my pledge on the grounds of temporary insanity\" once Johnny recovers -- even picking a friendly fight to celebrate.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=success; text=This was also a good moment.]\n\nI have to say that, even though the reaction of the underworld to the near-death experience of the Human Torch may have been overdone, I still laughed at the reactions of both ordinary citizens and the criminals.\n[ASSET: center | ItsABitMuch | width=50%]\n\nIt's melodrama dialed past eleven, but it works because the book fully commits to it.\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(What a gloriously over-the-top moment, <i>heh</i>!)]\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nLet's not fool ourselves:&nbsp; this story will <i>never</i> win any awards, and truth be told -- it was below average in so many ways.\n\nBut the fact of the matter was that it had enough laugh-worthy moments and genuine character beats that both managed to shine through the otherwise plentiful (and unhinged) plot elements and ultimately make it worth the read.\n\nThe Eel being the doofus who caused a crisis only the Human Torch could solve, together with an inventor who <i>really</i> could use some oversight, paired with the presence of an out-of-the-blue, critical TV commentator who turns nice in the end -- these all were merely the very poor dressing around the actual good moments.\n\nThe outrageously hilarious reactions of the underworld, together with the nobility of Johnny Storm, and the Thing's genuine affection for his teammate -- <i>these</i> are the sprinkled-in tidbits that turn what could have been an outright dumb plot into something that had moments of actual delight.\n\nIt's a hurriedly told story that will soon disappear from memory, but if you <i>ever</i> find yourself re-reading it, you can at least chuckle at some of the panels and also appreciate how <i>Strange Tales</i> is finally giving the Human Torch and the Thing some downright decent moments that the <i>Fantastic Four</i> magazine doesn't always have room for.\n\nBefore I close, I must say that I like the fact that the FF show up from time to time in this magazine, as they're a huge part of Johnny's life, but it's getting harder with each issue to ignore the fact that the police in Glenville sometimes make it appear as if he's the only one living in the town, when in reality, he lives with his sister, another full-fledged member of the Fantastic Four.\n\nDo they ever pay any attention to Sue Storm at <i>all</i>?&nbsp; Half the FF lives right <i>there</i>, and yet the only one the police or the news ever talk about is Johnny.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I <i>understand </i>this is a Human Torch solo feature, but still...!)]&nbsp; It's as if Glenville forgets she exists the moment Johnny lights up.&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; text=Perhaps she should be less invisible from time to time!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n<i>What!</i>  No Doctor Strange in this issue?\n[BR] I guess we weren't actually promised an appearance in the very next issue -- only \"future\" issues.",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] Scripter \"Joe Carter\" is the pseudonym for Jerry Siegel, who likely needs no introduction, being one half of the pair that created the character of Superman.\n\n[HANG0] Page 13 indicates that commentator Braddock gave Johnny a color TV set to make up for his derogatory comments about Johnny.&nbsp; Even though color televisions had been invented decades before, having one in 1963 was indeed early adoption, as most of the broadcasts on the networks were still in black-and-white."
  ],
  "highlight": "A flimsy Human Torch tale held together by sheer enthusiasm, a dash of altruism, and the kind of Silver Age audacity that somehow makes the whole thing work."
}